Every clinician should use an NSAID at a lowest-effective-dose during a shortest-needed-duration.
If a clinician uses an NSAID for a patient then the clinician should use the NSAID cautiously for the patient.
If a patient is an older-adult and a clinician uses an NSAID for the patient then the clinician should particularly use the NSAID cautiously for the patient.
If a patient has a cardiovascular-comorbidity or has a chronic-renal-failure and a clinician uses an NSAID for the patient then the clinician should particularly use the NSAID cautiously for the patient.
If a patient has a previous-gastrointestinal-bleeding and a clinician uses an NSAID for the patient then the clinician should particularly use the NSAID cautiously for the patient.
